#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
gpu-monitor - one nvtop-style view across many machines.

Reads a host list, probes every host in parallel over ssh, and renders the
combined result: an nvtop-style block per GPU plus a single process table
spanning the whole fleet.

Design notes
------------
Persistent sessions: hosts are named as they appear in ~/.ssh/config, so
ProxyJump, User and ControlMaster settings there apply. ControlMaster options
are also passed explicitly, so a host added to hosts.txt but absent from
ssh_config still gets a persistent multiplexed connection instead of a fresh
handshake every refresh.

Silence on failure: a host that is down, hung, or awaiting authentication
(tailscale login, missing key, 2FA) is dropped from the display entirely.
Two things make that reliable:

  * `ConnectTimeout` bounds only the TCP connect. An ssh sitting at a
    tailscale "visit this URL" prompt is already connected, so the whole
    invocation gets a hard wall-clock timeout as well.
  * A stalled ssh behind a ProxyJump spawns a process chain, so the probe
    runs in its own session and the whole process group is killed on
    timeout. Otherwise every refresh leaks ssh children.

Failure backoff: probing a dead host costs a full timeout. Failed hosts are
retried on a long interval so they never stall the refresh loop, and that
state is persisted so one-shot runs do not each start cold.

Layout: every GPU is a panel. The grid shape follows the device count --
rows = trunc(sqrt(devices)), columns = ceil(devices / rows) -- and panels fill
vertically first. The process table takes the height that is left and scrolls.
"""

